# RULE 51 — MEMORY BANK + EXECUTION MODE (owner 2026-09-08)

Owner (verbatim, on losing hours to agents re-deriving forgotten logic and stalling on passive
re-verification): the memory/passivity problem gets solved the same way `docs/bus/` already
solves cross-seat coordination — write it down where the next agent (or the next hour of this
same agent) is forced to read it, and strip the option to stall on "just checking."

## 1. The Memory Vault — `docs/MEMORY_BANK.md`

**Already exists and is already live** (Cursor's settlement-reconciliation effort, 2026-09-07/08 —
read it before touching settlements, reversals, or the fast-merge method). This rule generalizes
its own stated requirement ("Any agent that builds complex logic... MUST record it here before
ending its session") from that one effort to **every seat, every module**: if a session lands
complex logic — an engine, a root-cause class fixed across multiple files, a locked architectural
decision — it goes into `docs/MEMORY_BANK.md` **before the session closes**, not after.

Append under the existing top-level headings (`## Active Architectural Decisions` / `## Known
Quirks & Blockers` / `## Next Immediate Milestones`); a seat working a different module namespaces
its own sub-heading (`## Active Architectural Decisions — <Module> (<seat>, <date>)`) rather than
editing another seat's prose, same discipline as `docs/audit/GUARD-WORKORDERS.md`. **Read the file
fresh (`git show origin/main:docs/MEMORY_BANK.md`) immediately before editing it** — this repo
checkout is genuinely shared across concurrent seats; a plain overwrite here can destroy another
seat's real, in-progress entries (see the Known Quirks entry this rule's own author added after
almost doing exactly that).

Structure is guarded (`scripts/verify-memory-bank-structure.mjs`, verify-step 10827) — the file
must exist and keep its 3 required section headers. Whether a specific entry is *worth* including
stays judgment, not guardable; the guard only stops the file (or its section headers) from
disappearing outright.

`docs/CLAUDE.md` §13 lists it first among required reads for exactly this reason — read it before
touching a module you didn't just build yourself.

## 2. Execution mode — you are hired to build, not to audit

This restates and sharpens `00-IH35-LAW.mdc` rules 1/5/14 (already binding) because it is worth
saying in the specific words that stop the specific failure mode observed:

1. You are hired to **WRITE, FIX, and BUILD** code. You are not an auditor, and a finding without a
   fix is not the deliverable — fix it in the same block, in your own lane.
2. Do not spend multiple turns "verifying," "analyzing," or "confirming" once you have enough
   information to act. Verification that exists only to justify not yet starting is the failure
   mode this rule exists to stop — not the local-gate/typecheck/guard-selftest checks that are
   required plumbing for shipping a mergeable PR (those stay mandatory, per Rule 25/29; keep them
   quick and in the background, don't narrate them as the work itself).
3. If you find a bug or a missing implementation while working, do not just report it — fix it in
   the codebase in the same session, same as any other finding in your own lane.
4. Before starting complex or previously-built logic (a reconciliation engine, a posting flow, a
   migration class), read `docs/MEMORY_BANK.md` for how it was done, read the actual code, and
   execute. Do not ask permission to begin — Rule 1's "the live thing, not a plan about it" already
   covers this; this rule exists so the instruction survives being said only once in chat.

## 3. The bus as a progress gate

Every board/outbox entry that closes a session or a block states **what files it changed**, in the
same evidence block Rule 30/DoD already requires (`GUARD:`, `LIVE PROOF:`). An entry that only
verified something and changed nothing says so plainly (`Files Modified: none — verify-only pass`)
rather than reading like a fix — that phrase is the tripwire: if the next agent (or Jorge) sees
"Files Modified: none" on a row that was supposed to be a fix, that is the signal the seat stalled,
not that the work is done.
